{
  "result": "Every morning, before the city remembered its own name, a woman in a blue coat walked to the river and counted the bridges. There were always seven. She knew this. The bridges knew this. Still, she counted, because some things deserved to be noticed more than once in a lifetime.\n\nNobody asked her why. Cities are generous that way: they allow a person one small mystery, provided it does not interrupt traffic. The baker nodded through his window. A cyclist rang a bell. Somewhere, a delivery driver argued with a door that had mistaken stubbornness for a useful security feature.\n\nOne Tuesday, there were eight bridges. The new one was narrow and green, with railings warm as sleeping cats. It crossed no water. Instead, it stretched between two patches of sunlight beneath a willow tree. The woman counted again, slowly this time. Then she sat down and considered the possible implications.\n\nPerhaps the world had finally decided to show its unfinished work. Perhaps someone had wished very carefully. Or perhaps bridges, like mushrooms, occasionally appeared when conditions were right. She had no training in impossible architecture. What she did have was an apple, a free morning, and reasonably comfortable walking shoes.\n\nShe crossed. On the other side, nothing seemed different, except that she could suddenly remember every kindness she had forgotten: a borrowed umbrella, a patient answer, a hand steadying her bicycle. Behind her, the green bridge vanished. Ahead, the baker opened his door. She bought two loaves instead of one.",
